# SMART Health Check-in 1.0 — single-page specification: outline

Conventions used in this outline:

- `(N)` = normative section; `(I)` = informative; `(EX)` = examples block.
- Conformance verbs follow RFC 2119 / RFC 8174 (SHALL, SHOULD, MAY, MUST NOT, …).
- Each binding section is intended to end with a one-row-per-rule conformance
  checklist suitable for a certification suite.
- Architectural framing: the spec describes (1) a transport-neutral clinical
  content model, (2) a **same-device presentation flow** over W3C Digital
  Credentials API using direct `org-iso-mdoc`, and (3) a **cross-device kiosk
  flow** that builds on the same-device presentation flow by wrapping it with
  an additional pointer/relay envelope. The kiosk flow is *not* a separate
  protocol layer; it reuses the same-device flow on the patient's phone.

---

## Front matter

0. **Title, status, editors**
   0.1 Title block (e.g. *SMART Health Check-in 1.0 over W3C Digital Credentials API*)
   0.2 Document status, version, publication date, change log
   0.3 Editors / contributors / IPR statement
   0.4 Copyright + license (CC-BY for the document)

---

## 1. Introduction (I)

1.1 Abstract — one paragraph naming the parties, the artifact set, and the wire pieces being standardized
1.2 Scope — what this profile fixes so multiple EHRs and multiple wallets interoperate
1.3 Out of scope — issuance, longitudinal storage, EHR write-back, identity proofing, payments
1.4 Reading guide — which sections are normative vs informative; how to certify conformance against them
1.5 Document conventions
1.5.1 RFC 2119 / RFC 8174 keywords (SHALL, SHOULD, MAY, MUST NOT, …)
1.5.2 JSON / CBOR / CDDL / COSE / HPKE notation
1.5.3 Byte-string presentation (hex, base64url-no-pad)
1.5.4 Pseudocode and example dialect
1.6 Terminology — Verifier / Requester, Wallet / Responder, Holder, Browser / User Agent, Credential Manager, Artifact, Item, Profile family, Request item id, Kiosk creator, Submission service, Phone presenter, Completion display
1.7 References
1.7.1 Normative — RFC 2119, RFC 8174, RFC 7515 (JWS), RFC 9180 (HPKE), RFC 8949 (CBOR), RFC 8152/9052 (COSE), ISO/IEC 18013-5 mdoc, W3C Digital Credentials API, FHIR R4 (4.0.1), SMART Health Cards
1.7.2 Informative — OpenID4VP, IETF DCQL, US Core IG, CARIN IG, mDL Annex C

## 2. Purpose, problem statement, design goals (I)

2.1 Use cases
2.1.1 Same-device patient portal check-in
2.1.2 In-person front-desk kiosk check-in
2.1.3 Pre-visit intake from a patient phone
2.1.4 Insurance verification (CARIN-style coverage)
2.1.5 Health summary share for prior-auth or referrals
2.2 Why a check-in protocol vs. plain credential issuance/presentation
2.3 Goals
2.3.1 Transport-neutral clinical content
2.3.2 Per-item user consent
2.3.3 FHIR-native selectors (canonicals, profile families)
2.3.4 Many-to-many fulfillment (one artifact ↔ many items)
2.3.5 Interop across multiple EHRs and multiple wallet platforms
2.3.6 Layerable trust (origin → reader → issuer → clinical content)
2.4 Non-goals
2.5 Threat model summary (cross-references §10)

## 3. Architectural overview (I, with normative pointers)

3.1 Two payload domains: clinical content vs. presentation transport
3.1.1 Clinical content domain — the SMART JSON request/response (transport-neutral)
3.1.2 Presentation transport domain — direct `org-iso-mdoc` over W3C DC API
3.2 Two end-to-end flows
3.2.1 Same-device presentation flow (verifier page ↔ wallet on the same device)
3.2.2 Cross-device kiosk flow — a desktop creator and a phone presenter, where the phone re-enters the same-device presentation flow after resolving a pointer
3.3 Roles and component contracts (protocol-level only)
3.3.1 Requester / Verifier (the relying party)
3.3.2 Browser / User Agent (DC API surface, origin assertion)
3.3.3 Wallet / Responder (consent, response construction, signing)
3.3.4 Holder data source (wallet-internal; abstracted out of protocol)
3.3.5 Kiosk creator
3.3.6 Submission / relay service (untrusted transport)
3.3.7 Phone presenter (re-uses Verifier and Wallet flows on the patient's phone)
3.3.8 Completion display (the kiosk desktop receives the encrypted submission)
3.4 Sequence diagrams
3.4.1 Same-device presentation flow
3.4.2 Cross-device kiosk flow (highlighting the call back into the same-device flow on the phone)
3.5 Trust boundaries (forward reference to §7)
3.6 Design principles (each principle restated as a normative SHALL/SHOULD)
3.6.1 One docType, one namespace, one stable element
3.6.2 No requester identity in clinical request body
3.6.3 FHIR canonicals where they fit
3.6.4 No local topic vocabularies when FHIR terms exist
3.6.5 Response forms expressed as media types
3.6.6 Artifact-centered response with per-item status
3.6.7 Explicit FHIR version on raw FHIR JSON
3.6.8 Default-to-retention for clinical workflows
3.6.9 Crypto agility via profile registry, not in-band negotiation

