## 7. Trust framework (N)

7.1 Origin trust
7.1.1 Browser-asserted web origin (when DC API exposes it)
7.1.2 Wallet-side privileged-caller / browser-trust policy (package + signing-cert fingerprint where applicable; deployment-defined)
7.1.3 Behavior when origin cannot be authenticated
7.2 Reader / Verifier trust
7.2.1 Optional `readerAuth` COSE_Sign1 over `ReaderAuthentication`
7.2.2 Reader certificate chain (`x5chain`) + trust anchor policy
7.2.3 Wallet handling of unsigned vs signed reader requests
7.3 Issuer / device-attestation trust (mdoc binding)
7.3.1 MSO (issuer-signed) trust anchors — IACA-style or registry-based
7.3.2 Device key proof of possession
7.3.3 "Self-attested wallet" model (no external issuer trust required)
7.4 Source trust on clinical content
7.4.1 SMART Health Card chain of custody (signed credential payload)
7.4.2 Raw FHIR JSON treated as patient-mediated (binding by transport, not provenance)
7.5 Identifier scoping & uniqueness (request id, response id, artifact id)
7.6 Out-of-band trust establishment (deployment policy)

## 8. Same-device presentation flow over `org-iso-mdoc` (N)

8.1 Identifiers (registered in §13)
8.1.1 Protocol id `org-iso-mdoc`
8.1.2 mdoc `docType` `org.smarthealthit.checkin.1`
8.1.3 Namespace `org.smarthealthit.checkin`
8.1.4 Stable element `smart_health_checkin_response`
8.1.5 Request carrier claim `org.smarthealthit.checkin.request` (in `requestInfo`)
8.1.6 HPKE suite & COSE alg
8.2 Verifier-side request construction
8.2.1 SMART JSON serialization for `requestInfo`
8.2.2 `ItemsRequest` shape (docType, nameSpaces, requestInfo)
8.2.3 `intentToRetain` default = `true` (override conditions)
8.2.4 `DeviceRequest` v1.0 shape; v1.1 `readerAuthAll` not used
8.2.5 Tag-24 wrapping invariant
8.2.6 (Optional) per-DocRequest `readerAuth`
8.2.7 HPKE recipient keypair
8.2.8 `encryptionInfo` CBOR construction (`["dcapi", {nonce, recipientPublicKey}]`)
8.2.9 `navigator.credentials.get(...)` argument (base64url-no-pad fields)
8.2.10 (EX) full request bytes ladder
8.3 SessionTranscript construction (both sides SHALL compute identically)
8.3.1 `dcapiInfo = CBOR([encryptionInfoBase64Url, origin])`
8.3.2 `handover = ["dcapi", SHA-256(dcapiInfo)]`
8.3.3 `SessionTranscript = CBOR([null, null, handover])`
8.3.4 Origin acquisition rules and platform-neutral requirements
8.4 Wallet-side request handling
8.4.1 Recover and validate `ItemsRequest`
8.4.2 Decode SMART request JSON from `requestInfo`
8.4.3 Recompute SessionTranscript
8.4.4 Verify optional `readerAuth`
8.4.5 Run consent UI (per-item user choice)
8.5 Wallet-side response construction
8.5.1 Build SMART response JSON
8.5.2 `IssuerSignedItem` (digestID, random, elementIdentifier, elementValue)
8.5.3 Tag-24 wrap; SHA-256 digest
8.5.4 MSO contents + tag-24 payload + COSE_Sign1
8.5.5 `DeviceAuthentication` payload (with SessionTranscript)
8.5.6 Device COSE_Sign1
8.5.7 `DeviceResponse` envelope
8.6 HPKE encryption
8.6.1 Suite (`DHKEM(P-256, HKDF-SHA256)`, `HKDF-SHA256`, `AES-128-GCM`)
8.6.2 `info` = SessionTranscript bytes
8.6.3 `aad` = empty
8.6.4 `enc` (encapsulated key) format
8.6.5 `dcapiResponse` CBOR envelope (`["dcapi", {enc, cipherText}]`)
8.6.6 Outer DC API wrapper (`{"protocol":"org-iso-mdoc","data":{"response":...}}`)
8.7 Verifier-side processing
8.7.1 HPKE-open
8.7.2 Walk `DeviceResponse`
8.7.3 Verify `issuerAuth` (cert chain → trust anchor)
8.7.4 Re-hash `IssuerSignedItem` and compare against MSO digest
8.7.5 Verify `deviceSignature` against MSO `deviceKey`
8.7.6 Decode SMART response JSON; apply §6.6 cross-checks
8.8 Required validation checklist (verifier)
8.9 (EX) Annotated end-to-end byte capture (links to fixtures in Appendix D)

## 9. Cross-device kiosk flow (N)

> The kiosk flow builds on the same-device presentation flow defined in §8.
> The desktop creator hands the patient a pointer to a signed, encrypted SMART
> request; the patient's phone resolves and decrypts the pointer, runs §8 on
> the phone, and returns an encrypted SMART response back to the desktop.

9.1 Goals
9.1.1 Desktop staff initiation; phone-side presentation
9.1.2 Pointer-only QR (no FHIR or response keys on the wire)
9.1.3 Untrusted transport assumption
9.2 Roles
9.2.1 Kiosk creator (signs and encrypts the SMART request)
9.2.2 Submission / relay service (untrusted)
9.2.3 Phone presenter (decrypts and runs §8 in the same browser)
9.2.4 Completion display on the kiosk desktop
9.3 Cryptographic suite for the wrapper
9.3.1 Creator signature: ES256 compact JWS, `typ=smart-health-checkin+kiosk-request+jws`
9.3.2 Request encryption: ECDH-P-256 + HKDF-SHA-256 + AES-256-GCM
9.3.3 Submission encryption: same suite to ephemeral desktop key
9.3.4 Salt/info conventions (`KIOSK_REQUEST_INFO`, `KIOSK_RESPONSE_INFO`)
9.3.5 AAD = UTF-8 `requestId`
9.4 `KioskRequestPayload` JWS body (signed by creator)
9.4.1 `v`, `iss`, `aud`, `requestId`, `createdAt`, `expiresAt`
9.4.2 `submitTo` (transport descriptor)
9.4.3 Embedded `smartRequest` (full SMART request JSON)
9.4.4 `encryptRequestTo` (submission-service KEM material)
9.4.5 `encryptResponseTo.desktopPublicKeyJwk` (per-request ephemeral)
9.4.6 `constraints.maxPlaintextBytes` (negotiation, normative ceiling rule)
9.4.7 `minter.keyId` (creator key id)
9.4.8 Canonical-JSON signing rule
9.5 `EncryptedKioskRequest` envelope
9.5.1 Field set; content-type id; size limits
9.5.2 Storage shape (opaque to relay)
9.6 QR / pointer URL
9.6.1 Format `#r=<requestId>` (no payload data on the wire)
9.6.2 Privacy rationale
9.7 Phone resolution and re-entry into §8
9.7.1 Pointer fetch via provider
9.7.2 Decrypt with submission-service key
9.7.3 JWS verification against trusted creator key set
9.7.4 Bind decrypted payload to URL pointer (`requestId` match)
9.7.5 Run §8 same-device presentation flow against the embedded SMART request
9.8 Submission ciphertext from phone → desktop
9.8.1 Plaintext shape (`requestId`, `submittedAt`, `payload`)
9.8.2 Size enforcement against `maxPlaintextBytes`
9.8.3 ECDH to `encryptResponseTo.desktopPublicKeyJwk`
9.8.4 SHA-256 attestation
9.9 Desktop completion processing
9.9.1 Subscribe to provider for `requestId`
9.9.2 Download blob; decrypt locally
9.9.3 Validate request binding; render artifacts
9.10 Replay, expiration, and abuse considerations
9.10.1 Single-use guidance
9.10.2 `expiresAt` enforcement
9.10.3 Clock-skew tolerance
9.10.4 Out-of-band correlation hazards
9.11 Provider abstraction (informative — what the relay can/cannot see)
9.12 (EX) End-to-end kiosk flow with fixture URLs

